# Fixed 2026-11-20, defect 2522's row 1 *a jump through a buffer J never
# saw*, lane-signals (panel 251's R1: it owes words of its own); pinned as a
# known cost by the author's ruling B of 2026-11-20, lane-signals-2: a
# correct program saves a buffer, copies it, and its handler jumps out of
# itself through the copy; the signal over, it calls the function from far
# deeper.
# Why it is refused: the copy is a buffer the runtime holds no record of, so
# it cannot tell this jump out of the handler from a jump that lands inside
# it, after which the function would run inside the signal, where neither
# the runtime's memory nor the C library's is safe. Running it here would be
# a bet on memory; robustness ranks first (CLAUDE.md § Precedence), so the
# refusal stands, a known cost told in true words.
# Symptom before lane-signals: refused with *was called from inside a signal
# handler: C called it while handling a signal*, false of this program (40
# runs of 40 on this Mac at `-O0` and `-O2`, 20 of 20 at `-O0` in Linux
# arm64). Its words say what the runtime knows: a jump it could not follow.
extern "fixedbugs-2522-a-jump-out-of-a-handler-through-a-copied-buffer.h"
    function copied_install(sig: i32) -> i32
    function copied_escape(sig: i32) -> i32
    function copied_call(cb: (function(i64) -> i64)) -> i64

extern "signal.h"
    constant SIGUSR1: i32

function answer(n: i64) -> i64
    return n + 1

function main()
    print("installed ", copied_install(sig: SIGUSR1))
    print("escaped ", copied_escape(sig: SIGUSR1))
    print("called ", copied_call(cb: answer))
